A||||11||88formula||Got my new 3.4-liter engine running in my Formula||||1||
Z||000000||88formula||07-22-2001||03:36 AM||||Got my Formula on the road. My home computer died and I was off line for a while. Since then I have put 170 miles on the new 3.4-liter engine. This thing rocks! Even though I have not given it more than 1/3 throttle because of the new engine I can tell this thing is going to have torrid acceleration. The low-end torque is impressive and the engine tachs up super fast. I can’t believe how smooth it is either. It has literally gobs of torque. The engine runs great!  <P>I had a lot of trouble with the clutch hydraulic system and even now with the modification I did to the clutch pedal it is not 100%. I’m working on that one. I have the Borla exhaust system and FOCOA headers. The sound I get from them is not what I expected. I wanted a deep low rumble like a v8 or like it used to sound only louder. Instead it sounds really different. I have never heard anything that sounds like it does now. I have no cat and I think there is an exhaust leak somewhere, which may contribute to it’s unique sound. I’m not sure I like the sound. It is wicked loud too. When I stab the throttle the engine jumps from idle to high rpm in a fraction of a second and the sound it makes when it does that is awesome, I like that part about it. It really makes people look when I do that. <P>The engine has so much torque and the throttle is so sensitive that it’s hard to start out without making the car lunge in each gear. I’m serious, compared to the relatively week low end power of my stock 2.8 this thing is a beast. It even pulls in fifth @ 40 mph without bogging. <P>There were a few issues though when I first got it running and there are a few more I need to sort out. Considering the fact I rebuilt just about everything on this car like the suspension, brakes, and engine I’m surprised that I have so few problems. When I first ran the engine I used the 3.4 injectors and it seems to run just a tad rich when it’s cold (not sure of that though yet), after it warms up it seems to correct itself. I need to get a hold of a scanner to see what the integrator and BLM is. I have a set of stock blueprinted 2.8 injectors that I tried to use on this motor. The motor pumps so much air that it wouldn’t even run with the 2.8 injectors, they couldn’t supply the fuel it needs. It would start and then die immediately unless I enriched the fuel mixture by tapping the throttle or using propane enrichment to keep it running so I switch it back to the 3.4 injectors for now. The roller rockers ended up hitting the valve covers so as a temporary fix I doubled up the gasket on the valve covers to get the necessary clearance. I’ll have to make some shims out of aluminum or something to get them to clear. I don’t want to loose the stock look of my engine. I want to keep the people at the track scratching their heads.<P>Anyway I can’t wait until I have about 1000 miles on this engine and it has been tuned, that is when I’ll probably bring it to the track and open it up, or should I say let the beast run free.     <BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||88formula||07-22-2001||03:42 AM||||For those who are saying yeah right this engine is far from a stock 3.4-liter engine.||||reg||1
Z||000002||87FieroGTx||07-22-2001||04:13 AM|||| I don't doubt you a bit, I saw your build up!!!<P>  Sounds like FUN!<P> Now if I can only git MooCow to trade me his 3.4 4speed manual I can have some fun with ya)||||reg||1
Z||000003||fiero56||07-22-2001||07:44 AM||||Sounds like you've got a good setup. <P>An e-mail from Krebb's list, not sure if your a member on there or not. Kind of long, but has some info.<P>----- Original Message -----<BR>From: david rodabaugh &lt;drodabaugh@babsim.com&gt;<BR>To: &lt;fiero@yahoogroups.com&gt;<BR>Sent: Friday, June 08, 2001 1:59 PM<BR>Subject: [FIERO] 3.4L Open Loop Idle Problems<P><BR>&gt; Friends,<BR>&gt;<BR>&gt; You may recall that, earlier in the week, I asked for those<BR>&gt; with running 3.4L conversion to comment upon open loop<BR>&gt; idle quality.  Almost universally, the response I got back<BR>&gt; was "Terrible!".  I was only interested in those actually<BR>&gt; running the 3.4L injectors, and those were the responses I<BR>&gt; got.<BR>&gt;<BR>&gt; It seems the problem is the same in all cases.<BR>&gt; AT COLD OUTSIDE TEMPERATURES (40F or less), until the<BR>&gt; ECM goes into closed loop operation, in which the ECM<BR>&gt; uses the oxygen sensor for feedback control of the air/fuel<BR>&gt; ratio, the idle lopes.  In some cases, the loping is severe<BR>&gt; enough to cause an engine stall.  Once the throttle is applied<BR>&gt; even a little bit, the engine seems to run rather well.<BR>&gt;<BR>&gt; THE PROBLEM<BR>&gt;<BR>&gt; The engine is running rather rich during open loop idling.<BR>&gt; The mixture is so rich that the engine bogs as the<BR>&gt; cylinders begin to misfire.  The ECM responds by opening<BR>&gt; the IAC wide, which immediately leans out the mixture,<BR>&gt; allowing combustion to begin again.  Of course, the RPM<BR>&gt; surges upward, and the ECM then dials the IAC back to<BR>&gt; a more closed position.  The mixture is then rich again,<BR>&gt; and the process repeats itself.<BR>&gt;<BR>&gt; But why is the mixture rich?  It would seem that that the<BR>&gt; 3.4L injectors (which flow 17 lbs/hour), either have different<BR>&gt; transient effects at small injector pulsewidths (as would be<BR>&gt; experienced at idle), or the ECM's settings at open loop<BR>&gt; idle, with 2.8L injectors, are simply too large for the bigger<BR>&gt; injectors.  I am guessing that the second is the reason,<BR>&gt; since in all cases, once closed loop operation begins,<BR>&gt; the engine idles quite well.<BR>&gt;<BR>&gt; I am not an ECM expert, but I am guessing that the open<BR>&gt; loop lookup tables enrich the mixtures as the CTS readings<BR>&gt; drop.  Thus, as outside temperatures get colder, and during<BR>&gt; a true cold start, the mixture is just too rich, plain and simple.<BR>&gt;<BR>&gt;<BR>&gt; POSSIBLE SOLUTIONS<BR>&gt;<BR>&gt; - Reduce the fuel pressure via an adjustable fuel pressure<BR>&gt; regulator.  Ed says that a pressure between 31 and 34 psi is<BR>&gt; frequently good.  Ed, is this the pressure with the engine off,<BR>&gt; or during idle?  This method is one that I will be pursuing when<BR>&gt; I complete my ongoing 3.4L swap.  However, at this fuel pressure,<BR>&gt; I will be *carefully* watching the fuel injector pulsewidth and<BR>&gt; other data from the my scan tool, at heavy throttle and loads.<BR>&gt; If I think that the engine is leaning out, I will raise the fuel pressure<BR>&gt; back up, and find some other route.<BR>&gt;<BR>&gt; -  New PROM.  This is possible, I suppose.  So far, there is<BR>&gt; one available alternative from Joe Wynman.  However, given the<BR>&gt; EGR-disabling feature of this chip, I personally will not use it.<BR>&gt;<BR>&gt; -  Dupe the ECM when cold.  This would entail wiring a separate<BR>&gt; resistor circuit in parallel with either the MAT or CTS (whichever<BR>&gt; is causing the open loop enrichment).  This would be switched<BR>&gt; on from the cockpit during the open loop warmup period.  It would<BR>&gt; simulate a warmer temperature than actually exists, thus<BR>&gt; leaning out the mixture.<BR>&gt;<BR>&gt; This approach is fraught with difficulties, however, just beyond the<BR>&gt; wiring:<BR>&gt;<BR>&gt; -  The driver would have to know when to use it; this means they<BR>&gt; would have to have a good understanding of what it does, and why<BR>&gt; it should be used.<BR>&gt; -  The driver would have to remember to turn it off after a couple of<BR>&gt; minutes.  If on the CTS circuit, failure to turn the duper circuit off<BR>&gt; would mean that the vehicle never enters closed loop operation.<BR>&gt;<BR>&gt; -  Just let the car warmup for a couple of minutes with you right<BR>&gt; foot on the gas.  This method is completely free, but requires<BR>&gt; some of your time.  If you're like me, though, you never leave for<BR>&gt; work any earlier than you have to, so this may not be viable   ;-)<BR>&gt;<BR>&gt; Dave<BR>&gt;<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 07-22-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||88formula||07-22-2001||11:51 AM||||Fiero 56, that is an interesting article, thanks for that information. My engine doesn’t lope or idle up and down when it is cold it just idles at a relatively steady 1500 rpm. It does idle a little erratic and it does occasionally misfire until it goes into closed loop. <P>87 FieroGTx anytime you want to go to the track or something I would be more than up to the challenge. <BR>||||reg||1
